  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Warming trend through the rest of the week.\n\n- Wildfire smoke will continue to impact the region until further notice.\n\n- Near-critical to critical fire weather expected due to hot, dry and \nbreezy conditions today through Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure will maintain hot and dry conditions across western Montana \nand north-central Idaho into early next week. Temperatures will climb \nsignificantly today and Saturday, with lower elevations reaching the 90s \nand localized spots exceeding 100 degrees. The ridge of high pressure will \nflatten slightly through Saturday, allowing a noticeable increase in \nafternoon winds. The combination of intense heat, very low humidity, gusty \nwinds, and dry vegetation will create critical fire weather conditions, \npeaking today through Saturday. Red Flag Warnings are in effect, with the \nhighest concern focused across southwest and west-central Montana, as well \nas the Palouse and Hells Canyon regions of north-central Idaho.\n\nWildfire smoke remains the primary forecast challenge, significantly \nimpacting air quality and visibility across western Montana and north \ncentral Idaho. Strong nighttime inversions will trap smoke in valleys \neach morning. While afternoon heating will mix the lower atmosphere and \ntemporarily improve surface visibility, these breezier conditions will \nsimultaneously increase fire activity and generate more smoke aloft. The \ndensest surface smoke is expected to concentrate over northwest Montana, \nexperiencing only marginal afternoon clearing before settling again tonight\n into Saturday morning. Notably, areas under persistent, thick smoke may \nexperience suppressed atmospheric mixing, potentially keeping afternoon \nwind gusts and high temperatures locally lower than currently forecast.\n\u00a0\u00a0\nA slight increase in moisture from the south will bring a low chance of \nisolated thunderstorms from Friday afternoon through the weekend, primarily\nacross southwest Montana and Lemhi County. Because the air near the \nground is exceptionally dry, any rain will likely evaporate before \nreaching the surface, introducing a critical risk for dry lightning \nignitions and sudden, erratic wind gusts between 40 and 55 mph near any \nstorms.\n\nLooking ahead to the middle and end of next week, confidence is \nslowly increasing that moisture will return to the region. However, \nthis moisture is unlikely to produce widespread, soaking rainfall and \nwill instead primarily increase thunderstorm chances. Of higher confidence\n is a return of temperatures closer to seasonal normals, in the mid to \nupper 80s. A major weather pattern change, such as a strong, deep storm \nsystem from the Pacific Northwest, is necessary to significantly suppress \nfire activity and clear the smoke. Current forecasts show only a 20 to 30 \npercent chance of such a system arriving by the middle to late portions of \nnext week.",
